# --- Nix ---
result
result-*

# --- Rust ---
target/
# Cargo.lock IS tracked. This workspace produces binaries, not a library for
# others to depend on, so a reproducible dependency set is the whole point —
# the same reason nix/tamal exists.

# --- Build output ---
# `rutter android` puts the per-ABI shared libraries here; all regenerable.
build/
*.apk
*.aab
*.keystore

# --- Chart data ---
# Charts are large, often licensed, and never ours to redistribute. `rutter
# charts` writes wherever it is told; the source data belongs outside the
# repo either way.
#
# This deliberately does NOT ignore `charts/`, which is the crate that reads
# MBTiles. An earlier revision did, and would have silently swallowed it.
*.mbtiles
*.000

# ...except the test fixture, which is 32 KB, generated by this project's own
# pipeline from the GeoJSON beside it, and is the only thing proving the
# decoder agrees with tippecanoe rather than merely with itself.
#
# It is also the chart the APK bundles. `rutter apk` copies it into
# build/assets/ at build time rather than a second copy living under
# android/, so these bytes are tracked exactly once.
!charts/tests/data/*.mbtiles

# --- Weather data ---
# GRIB files are downloaded forecasts with a shelf life of hours.
*.grb
*.grb2
*.grib2

# --- Editor local history ---
.history
.history/

# --- Android signing ---
# A self-signed debug key, generated on first `rutter apk`. Worth nothing and
# regenerable, but a committed signing key is a bad habit to start.
build/debug.keystore
*.keystore
